      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Ireland Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "The objective of this study was to explore the trend and risk factors of the prevalence of hyperuricemia among post-menopausal women in Beijing over a ten years period."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD",
            "content": "This research was based on two cross-sectional surveys in post-menopausal women in Beijing. A total of 1881 post-menopausal women were included. Subjects answered a questionnaire and underwent a physical examination and blood test. Serum uric acid >357 μmol/L was diagnosed as hyperuricemia, and another diagnosis criterion (serum uric acid >416 μmol/L) was applied in these women."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Serum uric acid levels and hyperuricemia prevalence increased remarkably in the 10-year period. The prevalence of hyperuricemia increased more than 2-fold after being adjusted by age. Multivariate logistic regression models revealed hyperuricemia associated with age, education level, urban residents, alcohol consumption, hypertension, obesity, and dyslipidemia. With the increasing number of traditional cardiovascular risk factors (including hypertension, obesity, diabetes, and dyslipidemia), the risk of hyperuricemia increased significantly, and the accumulative effect of the factors on the risk of hyperuricemia was determined."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The prevalence of hyperuricemia was high and exhibited a remarkable, increasing trend in Beijing community-based, post-menopausal women. Better management of relative factors could help to prevent further increases in the burden of hyperuricemia in post-menopausal women in this region."
          }
        ]
      },
